    "body": "### Problem\n\nMulticursor cascade in Visual mode doesn't replay the same user input as in Normal mode.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n1. Create the following `init.lua`:\n\n    ```lua\n    _G.log = {}\n    _G.rhs = function()\n      local char = vim.fn.getcharstr()\n      local cursor = vim.api.nvim_win_get_cursor(0)\n      vim.api.nvim_buf_set_text(0, cursor[1] - 1, cursor[2], cursor[1] - 1, cursor[2], { char })\n      table.insert(_G.log, { char = char, cursor = cursor })\n    end\n\n    vim.keymap.set('n', 'gh', rhs)\n    vim.keymap.set('x', 'gh', rhs)\n    vim.keymap.set('x', 'gl', ':<C-u>lua _G.rhs()<CR>')\n\n    -- Prepare buffer for easier repro\n    vim.api.nvim_buf_set_lines(0, 0, -1, false, { 'aaa', 'bbb', 'ccc' })\n    vim.bo.modified = false\n    ```\n\n2. `nvim --clean -u init.lua`\n3. `vipQ` to start multicursor.\n4. Do one of the following:\n    4.1. `gh` - `x`. Applying this Normal mode mapping adds `x` at each multicursor.\n    4.2. `v` - `gh` - `x`. Applying this Visual mode mapping adds `x` only at main cursor and `\\27` (visualized as `^[`) at extra cursors. Executing `:=log` shows the following data that confirms that extra cursor were not replayed with `x` as user input:\n\n        ```lua\n        {\n          { char = \"x\",   cursor = { 3, 0 } },\n          { char = \"\\27\", cursor = { 1, 0 } },\n          { char = \"\\27\", cursor = { 2, 0 } }\n        }\n        ```\n    4.3. `v` - `gl` - `x`. The same outcome as the previous point.\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nMappings that require user input are replayed in the same way in both Normal and Visual modes.\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\nNVIM v0.13.0-dev-1824+g27ee55c09d\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\nNo\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nEndeavourOS Linux x86_64, 7.2.8-arch1-2\n\n### Terminal name/version\n\nGhostty 1.3.1-arch2\n\n### $TERM environment variable\n\nxterm-ghostty\n\n### Installation\n\nAppimage",
